## 1. Tags

The Gravwell Okta ingester writes to two tags, and both names are fixed — the
ingester will not let you change them.

| Tag | Macro | Contents |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `okta` | `$OKTA` | The Okta System Log. One JSON object per event: sign-ins, MFA challenges, policy evaluations, admin actions, application access. |
| `okta-users` | `$OKTA_USERS` | Okta user profile snapshots. One JSON record per user, re-published on every poll. |

That last point matters. `okta-users` is a **snapshot stream**, not an event
stream: the same user appears once per poll cycle. Any count over a window has to
collapse to one entry per user first, which is why every user query in this kit
starts like this:

```query
tag=$OKTA_USERS
| json
	id
	profile.department as department
| unique id
| stats count by department
```

Drop the `unique id` and your department counts multiply by the number of polls in
the timeframe.

## 2. Macros

The kit ships seven macros. Five are configuration; two carry query logic.

**Configuration**

| Macro | Type | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `$OKTA` | TAG | `okta` |
| `$OKTA_USERS` | TAG | `okta-users` |
| `$GRAVWELL_INSTANCE` | OTHER | Set this to your Gravwell base URL, no trailing slash. |
| `$OKTA_ALERT_SENDER` | OTHER | From address for alert email. |
| `$OKTA_ALERT_RECIPIENT` | OTHER | To address for alert email. |

**Query logic**

| Macro | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `$OKTA_RISK_SCORE` | Scores an enriched event 0-100 and buckets it into `_priority`. |
| `$OKTA_TUNING` | Drops allowlisted accounts and addresses; promotes denylisted ones to High. |

Both are commented line by line. Open either in the search GUI and read it — that
is the intended way to learn what the scoring does, and to change it.

Nothing else in this kit hides query logic behind a macro. Every query spells out
its own extraction, filtering, and enrichment, so you can read it top to bottom and
lift pieces into your own searches.

> One gotcha if you write your own macros: Gravwell's macro expander does not
> respect comments. A `$MACRO` reference inside a comment **inside that macro's own
> expansion** expands recursively and breaks the query. Refer to macros without the
> `$` in macro comments.

## 3. How a detection query is built

Every detection follows the same shape. Here is the whole of *Administrator Role
Assigned to an Okta User*, minus the field list, so you can see the skeleton:

```query
tag=$OKTA
| json
	eventType
	severity
	displayMessage as message
	outcome.result as outcomeResult
	actor.alternateId as actorAltId
	client.ipAddress as clientIp
	request.ipChain.[0].ip as requestIp
	published
	...
| grep -e eventType "user.account.privilege.grant"
| json
	target.[0].type as targetType
	target.[0].alternateId as targetAltId
	target.[1].type as target2Type
	target.[1].alternateId as target2AltId
| eval
	targetUser = "";
	if (match(targetType, "(?i)user")) { targetUser = targetAltId; }
	else if (match(target2Type, "(?i)user")) { targetUser = target2AltId; }
| lookup -r okta_eventTypes eventType eventType (category subcategory is_mod)
| eval
	actorUserNorm = strings_tolower(strings_replace(actorAltId, "@.+", "", -1));
	targetUserNorm = strings_tolower(strings_replace(targetUser, "@.+", "", -1));
| lookup -r okta_privileged_actor actorUserNorm account (actor_weight actor_label)
| lookup -r okta_protected_target targetUserNorm account (target_weight target_label)
| geoip clientIp.CountryName as srcCountry clientIp.City as srcCity
| geoip -r asn_db clientIp.ASNOrg as srcASNOrg
$OKTA_RISK_SCORE
$OKTA_TUNING
```

Six things worth knowing about that shape:

1. **The filter comes second.** It sits right after the `json` extraction, before
   the target parsing, the lookups, and the geoip calls, so the cheap test runs
   before the expensive work.

2. **`target` is an array.** Okta puts the objects an event acted upon into a JSON
   array whose contents vary: an `AppInstance` and an `AppUser` for an SSO event, a
   `User` and an `AuthenticatorEnrollment` for an MFA event, a `Rule` for a policy
   evaluation. There is no guarantee which slot holds the account, so the kit pulls
   the first two and picks whichever one is a user.

3. **Accounts are normalised before they are used as keys.** `actorUserNorm` strips
   the email domain and lower-cases what is left, so `Alice.Smith@example.com` and
   `alice.smith` both key as `alice.smith`. Everything that keys on an account —
   the allowlists, the denylists, the privileged-account weights — uses these.

4. **Okta's own geolocation wins; the local database is a fallback.** Okta
   resolves geography and network ownership at the edge, at the moment of the
   request, and attaches it as `client.geographicalContext` and `securityContext`.
   That is better data than a local database lookup, so the kit takes it whenever
   it is there. Only events that arrive without it get handed to `geoip` — the
   query builds a lookup key that is deliberately empty for everything else, so
   `maxmind` and `asn_db` are consulted for the stragglers rather than on every
   event. Each event carries a `geoSource` field saying which was used: `okta`,
   `okta+maxmind` when Okta gave the country and a local lookup filled a gap, or
   `maxmind` when Okta enriched nothing.

   On a typical tenant that is well under 1% of events falling back. Worth knowing
   when you read `srcASNOrg`: Okta writes operator names in lower case
   (`comcast cable communications  llc`) and `asn_db` in upper (`COMCAST-7922`),
   so the case tells you which source you are looking at.

5. **There is no render module.** The query ends at `$OKTA_TUNING`. That is
   deliberate: with no renderer the entry keeps every enumerated value it
   accumulated, and Gravwell hands all of them to the alert as fields. Add a
   `| table` and you would get only the columns you named.

6. **Detections that aggregate are named `[table]`.** A handful of detections —
   brute force, MFA bombing, session anomalies, rate limit abuse — cannot work on a
   single entry, because the signal *is* the aggregate. Those end in `stats` and a
   `table`, and their descriptions say which thresholds they use and where to change
   them.

## 4. Alerting: one router, many dispatchers

There is exactly one alert in this kit: **Alert - Okta - Alert Router**.

```
78 scheduled searches  ──dispatchers──▶  Alert - Okta - Alert Router  ──consumer──▶  Flow (email)
```

Each detection has one scheduled search. Every one of those scheduled searches is a
dispatcher on that single alert. Everything that should happen when a detection
fires is configured once, as a consumer of that alert.

**Why it is built this way.** Wiring notification per detection means 78 places to
change when you add a Slack channel, and 78 places to get wrong. With one router
you add a node to one flow, or add a second consumer, and every detection is
covered.

**Where alert names come from.** No query in this kit sets an alert title. The
dispatching scheduled search's name *is* the alert name. Rename the scheduled
search and you have renamed its alerts — no query edit needed.

**What arrives on the event.** For the raw-entry detections, every enumerated value
the query produced: `eventType`, `actorAltId`, `targetUser`, `clientIp`, `src`,
`srcCountry`, `srcASNOrg`, `outcomeResult`, `outcomeReason`, `risk_score`,
`_priority`, and the rest. Templates in the consumer flow reach them as
`{{ .event.Contents.eventType }}` and so on.

For the `[table]` detections, the columns of the aggregation arrive instead.

**Adding a destination.** Open *Flow - Okta - Alert Router - Email Notification*
and add a Slack, Teams, Mattermost, Google Chat, or PagerDuty node. Or add a second
flow as another consumer on the alert. Either way it applies to all detections.

**Everything ships disabled.** Enable a few scheduled searches, watch the volume,
tune, then enable more. The **Okta Threat Overview** dashboard runs the statistical
detections interactively so you can see what each would fire on before turning it
on.

## 5. Tuning out noise

Four lookup resources control noise across every detection at once. All four are
keyed on a **bare value** — an IP address, or a lower-cased username with the email
domain stripped.

| Resource | Columns | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `okta_user_allowlist` | `key,enabled,until_ymd,reason` | Drop events involving this account, as actor or target. |
| `okta_ip_allowlist` | `key,enabled,until_ymd,reason` | Drop events from this source address. |
| `okta_user_denylist` | `key,enabled,min_risk,reason` | Force events involving this account to at least High. |
| `okta_ip_denylist` | `key,enabled,min_risk,reason` | Force events from this address to at least High. |

`until_ymd` is a `YYYYMMDD` expiry compared against the event's `published` date.
Leave it empty for a permanent exception. `min_risk` gates promotion on the risk
score; leave it empty or `0` to always promote.

Worked example — a directory sync service account that trips half the detections:

```
key,enabled,until_ymd,reason
svc-okta-sync,1,,directory sync service account - permanently allowed
```

Add that row to `okta_user_allowlist` and every detection in the kit stops
reporting it. `enabled=0` parks a row without deleting it, so you keep the record
of why the exception existed.

Two more resources feed the risk score rather than filtering:
`okta_privileged_actor` weights accounts that perform actions, and
`okta_protected_target` weights accounts that are acted upon — more heavily, since
being the target of an admin action is worse than performing one.

## 6. Resources

| Resource | Rows | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `okta_eventTypes` | ~1000 | Every Okta System Log event type mapped to a category, a subcategory, and `is_mod`. This is the backbone of the category dashboards and the risk score. |
| `okta_privileged_actor` | small | Accounts whose actions carry extra risk weight. |
| `okta_protected_target` | small | Accounts that carry extra risk weight when acted upon. |
| `okta_user_allowlist` / `okta_user_denylist` | yours | Account-level noise control. |
| `okta_ip_allowlist` / `okta_ip_denylist` | yours | Address-level noise control. |

`okta_eventTypes` is worth browsing on its own. When you want to know what Okta
event types exist in a given area, search it rather than the API docs:

```query
tag=$OKTA
| json eventType
| lookup -r okta_eventTypes eventType eventType (category subcategory is_mod)
| stats count by category subcategory eventType
| sort by count desc
| table category subcategory eventType count
```

## 7. Dashboards

| Dashboard | Use it for |
| --- | --- |
| **Okta General Overview** | Start here. Ingest health on both tags, event volume by category and subcategory, sign-in geography, and a risk-priority health check. |
| **Okta Threat Overview** | Every statistical detection on one page. The fastest way to see whether anything is happening, and to sanity-check thresholds before enabling scheduled searches. |
| **Okta Investigation** | One actor, one target, and/or one source address across every System Log event. Driven by three dashboard variables; the actionables jump straight here. |
| **Okta Category Overview** | Drill into one `okta_eventTypes` category. |
| **Okta Event Type Overview** | Drill into the event types inside one category, split by outcome. |
| **Okta Actor/Target Overview** | Who did what to whom, inside one category. |
| **Okta Sign-In Location Overview** | Sign-in countries, cities, network operators, a point map, and the two geography-based detections. |
| **Okta User Overview** | Directory shape: totals, account status, division, department, job title, user type, manager. |
| **Okta User Authentication Overview** | Credential hygiene: lifecycle interval averages, MFA factor mix, stale passwords, unused accounts, locked accounts. |

## 8. Templates and actionables

The **Investigation** templates all take the same three variables, and all three
accept wildcards, so `*` in a slot means "any":

| Variable | Matched against |
| --- | --- |
| Okta Actor | `actor.alternateId`, falling back to `actor.displayName` |
| Okta Target | `target.alternateId` / `target.displayName`, either slot |
| Okta IP | `client.ipAddress`, falling back to the first hop of `request.ipChain` |

Three actionables — **Okta Actor**, **Okta Target**, and **Okta IP** — trigger on
email addresses and IP addresses in any search result and open the Okta
Investigation dashboard with that value filled in. That is the intended pivot from
"I see something odd in a search" to "show me everything about it".

## 9. Query library

Names follow a fixed pattern so the library sorts usefully:

```
AlertQuery - Okta - <category> - <subcategory> - <what it detects>
Search     - Okta - <area> - <what it shows> [renderer]
Template   - Okta - <area> - <what it shows> [renderer]
```

`AlertQuery` items are detections and have a matching scheduled search. `Search`
items are for looking at things. A `[table]`, `[chart]`, `[numbercard]`, or
`[pointmap]` suffix tells you the render module; a detection with no suffix ships
raw entries.

## 10. Getting started

1. **Install the Network Enrichment kit** if it is not already installed. This kit
   depends on its `maxmind` and `asn_db` resources.
2. **Onboard the data** with the Okta ingester. Confirm both tags are arriving with
   *Search - Okta - System - Ingest - Event Count by Tag*.
3. **Set the configuration macros.** `$OKTA` and `$OKTA_USERS` should already be
   right. Set `$GRAVWELL_INSTANCE`, `$OKTA_ALERT_SENDER`, and
   `$OKTA_ALERT_RECIPIENT`, and configure Account > Email Server > Email Server
   Settings.
4. **Look at Okta General Overview** to confirm the category breakdown is populated
   and the risk-priority card is not all one bucket.
5. **Look at Okta Threat Overview** over a week. Anything that lights up here is
   what the corresponding scheduled search would alert on.
6. **Enable a few scheduled searches**, starting with the ones whose event types you
   know are rare in your org: API token creation, identity provider changes, admin
   privilege grants, log stream changes.
7. **Enable the notification flow** once you are happy with the volume.
8. **Tune** using the allow and deny lists rather than by editing queries, so your
   changes survive a kit upgrade.

